joining me now is the former secretary  of labor under bill clinton  a berkeley economist professor robert  reich he's also the author of the book  the system  who rigged it how we fixed it uh robert  pleasure to have you on the show  we're about to be treated to another  round of we can't afford it from  born again deficit hawks in the  republican party who haven't cared  one bit about deficit spending under  donald trump putting their hypocrisy to  one side for a moment  do explain to our viewers why it is so  important for biden administration to go  big on spending  and why adding to the debt is not a big  deal right now  well first of all you say putting their  hypocrisy to one side if you put  hypocrisy to one side there's nothing  left i mean basically the republican  party today  true not true is the epitome of  hypocrisy i mean they gave a two  trillion dollar tax cut to the wealthy  uh and to big corporations  and they never talked about deficits  they never ever ever talked about debt  and now suddenly it's all about deficits  and debt once again  predictably that's happened every single  time a democratic administration has  come in  and they're doing it now even before the  democratic administration has come in  look you ask why is it so important to  spend now it's important to spend and we  understand this this is economics 101  because if you've got so many people  unemployed if you've got  basically a lot of a lot of businesses  that are going under one-third of small  businesses are no longer there  i mean you've got to rely on government  as the spender of last resort we  understood this we understood this  in the 1940s 50s 1960s richard nixon  of all people he said we're all  keynesians now  referring to the doctrine that i just  enunciated that we  do have to rely on government spending  uh and you don't  you don't worry about debt there is not  even a whiff of inflation in the air you  can't find inflation  anywhere you look under your under your  bed look in the closet  there is no inflation so let's not worry  about the possibility  of the economy overheating right now it  is critical  that we make these kinds of public  expenditures  to keep to help people survive that's  what we're talking about fundamentally  here we're talking about  survival well well put well put indeed  one bone of contention in this quote  unquote partisan stimulus debate that we  often report on in the media  has been federal funding for local and  state governments you talk about people  surviving it's often  at the grassroots community level  republicans have generally opposed most  of it but we're learning  uh the costs of that now first there's  the lack of local infrastructure to  administer  covert vaccines when they become  available and now you have this  situation where we're learning about for  example  the washington dc metro has a half a  billion dollar  budget shortfall if it doesn't receive  more federal funding it will need to  shed more jobs  cut bus routes close train stations  all of which impacts the larger  workforce amplify that across the  country  and it's a disaster even for  republican-run states republican-run  communities  well that has never stopped the  republican party before  i mean look at medicaid medicaid  expansion a lot of republican governors  said we don't want it  and then they were told well wait a  minute the federal government is going  to pick up  almost all of this expenditure and it's  your people who are going to be  given or at least have access to health  insurance you're critically important  and nevertheless the governors of  republican states said no we still don't  want it  look the the point is that state and  local governments are critical  in terms of fighting kovid they're  critical in terms of getting the economy  back on track  we're talking about millions of people  who are teachers  and police officers social workers fire  uh fighters  uh people who are first responders if  they don't get  if they can't keep their jobs and we've  already lost a lot of them because  states  can run deficits by their own state  constitutions almost every one of them  says no deficits so the federal  government has got to  help the states out for the for the sake  of the public  yeah robert what do you make of the  biden economics team of janet yellen at  treasury  near a tandem at the budget office are  they progressive enough  ambitious enough for your liking i mean  there's no sign of an elizabeth warren  or a bernie sanders in his cabinet not  yet at least  uh no there's no fire brand i mean i i  hope  and that somebody like elizabeth warren  or bernie sanders  gets there gets into the cabinet  although they're very valuable where  they are as well  let's make no mistake about that uh but  uh the the biden  administration the ins in cohen biden  administration  i think is going to be uh certainly had  the right values there's no question  about that  the question that to my mind is the same  as i asked and many people asked about  the obama administration  how aggressive are they willing to be  how bold are they willing to be  particularly  when they have to deal with a republican  congress uh and  unless those two georgia seats are  flipped on january 5th  uh the republicans mitch mcconnell are  still going to be in charge  of the senate yes  and of course joe biden has a lot of  plans if he can get that senate majority  in addition to continuing coveted  stimulus  he's proposed some other economic  proposals for working people  including raising the minimum wage to 15  and for giving  some amount of student loan debt  yesterday robert you tweeted quote  if we can cancel trillions in taxes for  a handful of billionaires  we can cancel student loan debt for 45  million  students do you think the biden plan for  student loan forgiveness goes  far enough uh what should he be doing on  this in your view  i think it could go farther he's talking  about up to ten thousand dollars  of student loan forgiveness uh i am i i  think  elizabeth warren hit it right maybe  fifty thousand uh and  graduated according to family income but  you see one of the advantages here when  you're dealing with federal student loan  debt  uh the president can do this  cancellation by executive order  he doesn't need to wait for mitch  mcconnell or the republicans if they  still have a kind of a veto  or power over the over the senate or  over the president's  agenda uh he can do this and it would be  a huge stimulus we have  a huge number of people obviously who  owe student debts  they are not just uh within years of of  having graduated  some of them are middle-aged some of  them in fact i ran into somebody  recently  uh a uh somebody who was on social  security  a retiree uh she said to me you know my  retirement checks are being my social  security checks are being garnished  by the federal government because i was  a co-signer of my daughter's student  debt my student loan debt uh 10 years  ago well this is absurd  uh particularly when we're going into  the uh the the  the double dip or or triple you could  say triple dip  of a for increasing covid and also  all of the other problems we've been  talking about no student debt can  and should be at least if not eliminated  entirely  up to 50 000  yeah completely agree uh one last  question before we run out of time i do  want to talk about billionaires for a  minute  uh specifically one billionaire tesla  ceo elon musk a few months about you  tweeted that he was a quote  modern day robber baron for cutting  workers wages while his own net worth  quadrupled to 100 million dollars  uh during the pandemic uh quadruple by  100 million dollars doing the panama he  responded by saying his workers get  stuck  so it evens out he called you a quote  modern day [ __ ]  uh putting aside the name calling for a  moment and the fact that so many  billionaires  are such big babies do you buy his  argument  that it all evens out with stock options  especially at a time of  rising inequality and billionaires like  musk getting richer  during this pandemic uh he's now the  second richest man  in the world right now uh and it doesn't  make any sense he has fought unions  he has fought safety features in his own  factories  uh he is not reporting on the degree of  covid but we have reason to believe that  there has been a lot of covid  uh he basically thumbed his nose at  public officials  who wanted to go slow with regard to  reopening factories he said i'm going to  reopen my factory anyway  you you can't play fast and loose uh  with with organized  with workers whose lives are at stake uh  who want to organize unions who want to  actually get some piece of the action  and i think that uh elon musk uh  well i i won't say he's a modern day of  [ __ ] he isn't uh he can he can call me  whatever names he wants  but i do think he's a he's a modern-day  robber baron because we're but we're  back  in the second gilded age we're back  where they where we were 120 years ago  with robber barons  who didn't really care about their  workers who didn't care about widening  inequality  didn't care about the consequences of  their spending  on politics and on the corruption of  government uh elon musk  is a representative of the modern robber  baron mentality  that infects so much of america today  i'm afraid you're right about that  there's definitely the infecting of  america today  robert reich we appreciate you taking  time out thank you so much for coming on  the show

